#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125286} :activity/id #uuid "3372fca1-80b5-5808-a2bd-c2a0e0659773" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049809 :topic/id #uuid "156d2779-eafb-5c80-bd7a-1ed2ab0b5ca6" :topic/math-academy-id 1937} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125730 :tutorial/content "We've already seen that if the function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b),$ then the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(a,b) = (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b),\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nz = f(a,b) + (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b).\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is differentiable at $(a,b),$ it can be approximated by its tangent plane at $(a,b)$ *provided that* $(x,y)$ is \"close\" to $(a,b).$ Thus, by replacing $z$ with $f(x,y)$ in the above equation, we get the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + (x-a)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a,b) + (y-b)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(a,b).\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the best linear approximation of the function $f(x,y)$ in an open neighborhood of $(a,b).$\n\nFinally, we can write the right-hand side of the above equation using the gradient vector $\\nabla f(a,b),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx  f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b) \\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b\\rangle\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ac689477-f228-5dcd-9ffa-18559bfead85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2414 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9a58c4d-a5c9-575a-90d9-009acca5ba22" :step/math-academy-id 10795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124255 :question/id #uuid "37734955-23b7-52d1-881d-0c77f672c35a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518199}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "0c37b1f0-332e-5bea-b9b7-317063ab4734" :step/math-academy-id 10796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124608 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124420 :question/id #uuid "839eb8fd-39b8-5f1f-b833-7134d39cdf2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504698}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"} :step/id #uuid "f38bdda7-c2e9-534b-8cd8-d22757c051a6" :step/math-academy-id 32184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124422 :question/id #uuid "54ac5be6-5b6a-58a3-a960-0d9f18e7fc88" :question/math-academy-id "e-8692"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518157}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"} :step/id #uuid "33df8c71-a53b-56e2-bd72-aceb78b19aa2" :step/math-academy-id 32300 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125852 :tutorial/content "So far, we've been using the fact that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b),$ then\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe can extend this result to higher dimensions. \n\nFor example, if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b, c),$ then \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle,\n$$\n\nfor all $(x,y,z)$ that lie in some open neighborhood of $(a,b,c).$\n\nMore generally, if a function $f(\\mathbf x)$ in the variable $\\mathbf x = (x_1, x_2. \\dots, x_n)$ is differentiable at the point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\Bbb R^n,$ then \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) \\approx f(\\mathbf x_0) +  \\nabla f(\\mathbf x_0) \\cdot (\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0)\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf x$ that lie in some open neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "683c6bf8-6cc9-5f40-afe2-0857ca3340c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5547 :tutorial/title "Linearization of Multivariable Functions in General"} :step/id #uuid "45087c9a-e754-5204-87c1-5e7e8df40c40" :step/math-academy-id 34419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125853 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x,y)=4-x^2-y^2$ at the point $P(1,1).$ We want to find the best linear approximation to this function, valid in a neighborhood of $P.$\n\nThe partial derivatives of $f$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-2x\\, & ⟹\\, &  & {f}_{x}(1,1)=-2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2y\\, & ⟹\\, &  & {f}_{y}(1,1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivatives are continuous functions at every point, which means that $f$ is differentiable everywhere, and\n\n$$\n\\nabla f (1,1) = \\langle -2, -2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best linear approximation of $f$ in a neighborhood of $(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+(-2)\\cdot (x-1)+(-2)\\cdot (y-1) \\\\  & =2-2x+2-2y+2 \\\\  & =6-2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the diagram below. Notice that in the neighborhood of $(x,y)=(1,1)$, the surface and its tangent plane approximation are very close.\n\n![](images/20/20f06226ec7c21583e56249ea2fe5c65c6127cef8a6e05ee4b1d7521ce49dc38.png)\n\n   \n\nTherefore, we can use this to approximate values of $f(x,y)$ that are close to $(1,1).$ For example, using our approximation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,1.01) & ≈6-2(1.01)-2(1.01) \\\\  & =1.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe actual value of $f$ at this point is $1.9598,$ so our approximation is pretty good!" :tutorial/id #uuid "eb2571f8-ee0b-571d-bacf-34a1e469da8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5593 :tutorial/title "Linearizing a Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "75f15315-a63f-503e-9c27-079061856ae0" :step/math-academy-id 34498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125554}}] :activity/title "Linearization of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124729}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420933} :activity/id #uuid "e616bf48-afee-5693-88d5-4ed73b51c3cc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049809 :topic/id #uuid "156d2779-eafb-5c80-bd7a-1ed2ab0b5ca6" :topic/math-academy-id 1937} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c5637de-9cd7-5f67-a225-8bc3edaa10f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361167} {:answer/id #uuid "71543430-b5a0-5270-904e-759c928c2fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361337} {:answer/id #uuid "c44dcf61-95dc-5835-9ab0-c46e40faab33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361720} {:answer/id #uuid "e6886e83-9bbe-5001-aaf4-2aaa990f8f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361866} {:answer/id #uuid "fa86c204-610a-5866-ba1b-3e096237ed19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361866} :answer-field/id #uuid "11250460-0d69-591d-a461-3a1f48f9b053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "52cca677-fb8e-5fcb-a6f2-9f28e4bd054c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98344" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(-3,-1,-2)=1, &  & {f}_{x}(-3,-1,-2)=4, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(-3,-1,-2)=-3, &  & {f}_{z}(-3,-1,-2)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-3,-1,-2)$ estimate the value of $f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-3,-1,-2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-3,-1,-2)+∇f(-3,-1,-2)\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩ \\\\  & =f(-3,-1,-2) \\\\  & \\,+⟨{f}_{x}(-3,-1,-2),{f}_{y}(-3,-1,-2),{f}_{z}(-3,-1,-2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-3.04,$ $y=-1.02,$ and $z=-2.03$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03) & ≈1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3.04+3,-1.02+1,-2.03+2⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,-0.02,-0.03⟩ \\\\  & =1-0.16+0.06+0.06 \\\\  & =0.96\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "271dc584-8067-5c79-8663-42f0cf534fe1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a5d7fe-a63b-5e32-949d-b3f2969e0db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420919} {:answer/id #uuid "3a60a799-4271-555e-b402-ed7491785238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-1-2x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420921} {:answer/id #uuid "9c37dd2d-538f-57a0-aa67-b6fb35e4d8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2+x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420923} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cdea38-23c3-571a-bf79-4dd2da2d05bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420924} {:answer/id #uuid "fe73bc79-52e6-53a8-9039-023077c29e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-1-2x-4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420921} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f2f20d-59aa-5f4b-b243-cfacb310155a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55146915-2d47-5b59-9386-37303589ba19" :question/math-academy-id "q-129741" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,0)+∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,0)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =2x{e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,0)=-2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & ={x}^{2}{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,0) & =⟨-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,0)+∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩ \\\\  & ≈1-2(x+1)+y \\\\  & ≈-1-2x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "428b58fe-7d44-5904-bbbf-8158fe8c3758"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3996a57d-3ca8-524e-b713-371dae9f92c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\pi -1-x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420920} {:answer/id #uuid "783e5bf5-dd91-511c-81fe-eca19c21cfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420922} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0f50fc-3f9e-56b0-874f-3d40ee14ed33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-2-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420925} {:answer/id #uuid "df1e2f42-abc0-5025-a335-6ed1280fc719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420926} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a0590c-c153-5bd3-ab0a-b20fc19d4881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420927} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6db9cae-9882-5bc9-bc06-4b3bc77a65e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "051e613f-42d1-5b65-a3d0-8638cacfa921" :question/math-academy-id "q-36746" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,\\pi )+∇f(0,1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,1,\\pi )=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(0,1,\\pi )=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(0,1,\\pi )=0, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(0,1,\\pi )=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,1,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,1,\\pi ) & =⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,\\pi ),{f}_{y}(0,1,\\pi ),{f}_{z}(0,1,\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,\\pi )+∇f(0,1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ac2d8892-990a-561c-b817-20d63c4e20b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87af71d8-c14b-5e93-8a58-f967155726ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124131} {:answer/id #uuid "8c132d2f-f0e1-55c6-8681-fe71e2237c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124132} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3bd8a9-f201-5d6b-bf76-51931b9ad32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124133} {:answer/id #uuid "d42ce0c3-890f-5ac1-a33f-8104d2a260ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124134} {:answer/id #uuid "46c19a80-75d5-5b32-b217-f47c15153172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124131} :answer-field/id #uuid "b665f142-dd59-559d-9742-e6ed101ab440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d1a53d9-3ef8-57e5-86e4-66648db12db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97877" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,-2,-1)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1)=2,\\,{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,-2,-1)+∇f(2,-2,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,-2,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.02,$ $y=-1.98,$ and $z=-1.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05) & ≈4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨2.02-2,-1.98+2,-1.05+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =4+(0.04-0.04+0.2) \\\\  & =4.2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de8e0d3a-aa28-5870-806b-fac6775a1e3e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ff1e57-06de-5f95-bbfb-858cc1b13fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263206} {:answer/id #uuid "3b04143b-57f8-52cd-a738-9fc5fdf9d65e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263525} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbbad2d-eebb-5dca-a3ea-e0c4084284e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263563} {:answer/id #uuid "431cea10-5e99-5fae-b2da-32b333bb11c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263591} {:answer/id #uuid "fcdaec9c-037d-5591-9664-14a70772a823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263525} :answer-field/id #uuid "3afc059a-63a4-57e9-911d-aba7f14a7f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f88dd94-3a57-5874-9049-b04644f1e878" :question/math-academy-id "q-129657" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.08,$ $y=1.93,$ and $z=3.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.08,1.93,3.05) & ≈6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.08-1,1.93-2,3.05-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.08,-0.07,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =6+6(0.08)+3(-0.07)+2(0.05) \\\\  & =6+0.48-0.21+0.1 \\\\  & =6.37\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f9443769-9265-54cf-81f2-24a4db8a375c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420932}}] :activity/title "Linearization of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420918}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489315} :activity/id #uuid "fd6c1f5e-0479-589e-8277-39b3bde8383f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049809 :topic/id #uuid "156d2779-eafb-5c80-bd7a-1ed2ab0b5ca6" :topic/math-academy-id 1937} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7d0aa5-2342-5d6f-a521-a62d6d54725c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x}{108}+\\frac{y}{54}+\\frac{z}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488506} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8e5783-e729-5001-b425-9bd95d7f94c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x}{108}-\\frac{y}{54}-\\frac{z}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488619} {:answer/id #uuid "52aebee7-a1be-5094-b50c-2fecab43978c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x}{108}-\\frac{y}{108}-\\frac{z}{108}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488628} {:answer/id #uuid "8b357635-83ca-53f3-be6f-efc2212bf65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{x}{108}+\\frac{y}{54}+\\frac{z}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488749} {:answer/id #uuid "bac0b568-47cf-5aed-a2d1-d6ae3ec5aafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x}{54}-\\frac{y}{54}-\\frac{z}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488619} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5948ebf-b36f-50ef-996a-235488992a7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d2fd7c0-6fec-5b30-98fe-3b0bf10ba9a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-65338" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,4,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,4,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,4,4)+∇f(2,4,4)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-4,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,4,4)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(2,4,4)=-\\frac{2}{{({2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2})}^{3/2}}=-\\frac{1}{108}, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-\\frac{y}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(2,4,4)=-\\frac{4}{{({2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2})}^{3/2}}=-\\frac{1}{54}, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-\\frac{z}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(2,4,4)=-\\frac{4}{{({2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2})}^{3/2}}=-\\frac{1}{54}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,4,4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,4,4) & =⟨{f}_{x}(2,4,4),{f}_{y}(2,4,4),{f}_{z}(2,4,4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{108},-\\frac{1}{54},-\\frac{1}{54}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,4,4)+∇f(2,4,4)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-4,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+⟨-\\frac{1}{108},-\\frac{1}{54},-\\frac{1}{54}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-4,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{108}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{54}(y-4)-\\frac{1}{54}(z-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x}{108}+\\frac{1}{54}-\\frac{y}{54}+\\frac{4}{54}-\\frac{z}{54}+\\frac{4}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x}{108}-\\frac{y}{54}-\\frac{z}{54}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "439f497b-63cd-56ed-a071-521a6753dfc6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489376}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2803179-deb8-5b5d-8dc3-c87b7f30ccbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404948} {:answer/id #uuid "00a43c2f-3146-563d-a70e-8ed6a8db819c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404949} {:answer/id #uuid "06c16dd4-4550-535b-882e-e29298a652c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404950} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf2d0ae-13a3-53c2-9b69-e902b7337d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404951} {:answer/id #uuid "311b8139-e8df-52d9-8b84-27e1171d5a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404952} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf12e78-7f08-5ce3-b44f-e89da0101f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02cd9397-a795-5b68-a5d4-f55d0d92f60a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98553" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-1{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇\\,f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+2(x+1)-2(y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5ad6acc3-0cf4-512b-9839-fcc4716b1315" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3caa2291-4781-5d49-9cf0-05616cc899e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263542} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2d1e6e-8f7d-5fad-ad7a-3806bd5dc59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263924} {:answer/id #uuid "707ba34c-bab5-5a04-b941-dc29d462b79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263973} {:answer/id #uuid "73de42ef-7102-53a5-ae70-a213df8590b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264008} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b5cd27-fbea-5768-a42b-ce72b13039ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263542} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc70e084-f962-56a8-b409-6b4140bd2930" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73178492-8508-56c4-9569-cdff37299fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129654" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{x}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,-1)+∇f(1,0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,0,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,-1),{f}_{y}(1,0,-1),{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.02,$ $y=0.1,$ and $z=-0.96$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.02,0.1,-0.96) & ≈1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1.02-1,0.1,-0.96+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.1,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =1-1(0.02)-1(0.1)+0(0.04) \\\\  & =1-0.02-0.1+0 \\\\  & =0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5e7f4e61-c58d-5ffd-a813-3c6ffee4ea99"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2731dfc7-d2c6-5c2c-a042-6a0654dbb592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈35+9x+12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488553} {:answer/id #uuid "699fa767-12e0-5e62-9b8f-2b731f1a39e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈41+6x+12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488677} {:answer/id #uuid "7a449c97-159a-5f59-a721-89db59ab69e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈23-9x-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488714} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fec697-0895-5a3c-96df-884cfd51b791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈35-9x+6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488806} {:answer/id #uuid "fb391dd4-bbf2-513b-9210-bd1b8336cab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈23+9x-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488553} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb4d8a0-1307-529a-b016-d7f1bd8d71e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a523061-b982-5542-a530-946bca7d0e33" :question/math-academy-id "q-98552" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-1.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-2,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-2,-3)+∇f(-2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x+2,y+3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-2,-3)=-19.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={y}^{2}\\, &  & ⇒\\,{f}_{x}(-2,-3)=(-3)^{2}=9, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =2xy &  & ⇒\\,{f}_{y}(-2,-3)=2(-2)(-3)=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-2,-3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,-3) & =⟨9,12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-2,-3)+∇\\,f(-2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+3⟩ \\\\  & =-19+⟨9,12⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+3⟩ \\\\  & =-19+9(x+2)+12(y+3) \\\\  & =35+9x+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d73be3e2-360c-5190-b3b2-da40bf0a43c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d1682a6-0223-5ed8-8277-0f5be1c651f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9.48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425837} {:answer/id #uuid "5c547bf0-6a15-5b93-836e-c0f81afbd41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425840} {:answer/id #uuid "c614f0d9-6fb7-53fc-b7ea-7df222acd263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425843} {:answer/id #uuid "ce98542a-f5c0-51c4-ab6c-242d4d9c6e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425845} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f80dc7-8dae-5243-9e68-a3715cc5329a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425843} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b6b4b5-7cfe-50c1-9f11-ac3785660947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e7afc5f-975b-58b4-8d28-e717181d277e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98349" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,3)=-9,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=x,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,3),$ estimate the value of $f(0.98,-0.01,3.1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,3)+∇f(1,0,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =-9+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,3),{f}_{y}(1,0,3),{f}_{z}(1,0,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,0,3)$, ${f}_{y}(1,0,3)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,0,3),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,0,3) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,0,3) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,0,3) & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.98,-0.01,3.1)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.98,y=-0.01$ and $z=3.1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.98,-0.01,3.1) & ≈-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨0.98-1,-0.01,3.1-3⟩ \\\\  & =-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.02,-0.01,0.1⟩ \\\\  & =-9-0.01-0.6 \\\\  & =-9.61\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e2dfefda-76b1-52f8-a7d5-688673b9ad6c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489369}}] :activity/title "Linearization of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488467}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518307} :activity/id #uuid "306b216a-6404-5818-a39c-7e75fa1559fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049809 :topic/id #uuid "156d2779-eafb-5c80-bd7a-1ed2ab0b5ca6" :topic/math-academy-id 1937} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518229 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87af71d8-c14b-5e93-8a58-f967155726ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124131} {:answer/id #uuid "8c132d2f-f0e1-55c6-8681-fe71e2237c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124132} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3bd8a9-f201-5d6b-bf76-51931b9ad32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124133} {:answer/id #uuid "d42ce0c3-890f-5ac1-a33f-8104d2a260ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124134} {:answer/id #uuid "46c19a80-75d5-5b32-b217-f47c15153172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124131} :answer-field/id #uuid "b665f142-dd59-559d-9742-e6ed101ab440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d1a53d9-3ef8-57e5-86e4-66648db12db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97877" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,-2,-1)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1)=2,\\,{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,-2,-1)+∇f(2,-2,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,-2,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.02,$ $y=-1.98,$ and $z=-1.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05) & ≈4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨2.02-2,-1.98+2,-1.05+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =4+(0.04-0.04+0.2) \\\\  & =4.2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "15d3249d-d1cd-53bd-a1e4-e6f226addfa8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518230}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67dfeaeb-1e41-58cc-9a73-4c453d503c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517679} {:answer/id #uuid "bead9479-8739-5826-ae34-4afcf877117d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517851} {:answer/id #uuid "d1051c05-0555-59bf-bd9a-560bd3386e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517881} {:answer/id #uuid "db9225d9-c497-562a-b106-0754baa0db31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517904} {:answer/id #uuid "e427de28-f06a-5b57-9189-a092c905fde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517851} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b62e85b-4101-530d-8082-303741e5d581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "72ddfe60-c39c-529d-b7e5-b5ef0ab41db5" :question/math-academy-id "q-98343" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,0,4)=-5,\\,{f}_{x}(3,0,4)=3,\\,{f}_{y}(3,0,4)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(3,0,4)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ estimate the value of $f(2.97,-0.02,3.98).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,0,4)+∇f(3,0,4)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-0,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =f(3,0,4)+⟨{f}_{x}(3,0,4),{f}_{y}(3,0,4),{f}_{z}(3,0,4)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.97,-0.02,3.98)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.97,$ $y=-0.02,$ and $z=3.98$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.97,-0.02,3.98) & ≈-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2.97-3,-0.02,3.98-4⟩ \\\\  & =-5+⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.03,-0.02,-0.02⟩ \\\\  & =-5-0.09+0.02-0.04 \\\\  & =-5.11\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22421f8f-e0e6-59b6-a0cb-85c1a6a1fe83" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29960ef5-732d-5bda-992a-c2410df10260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517563} {:answer/id #uuid "31371fc5-3db5-5f52-a902-57193843002d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517582} {:answer/id #uuid "410f0bc0-fa46-543e-a594-c94e9437eab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517617} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9c2861-2dbc-526f-80ca-6ca1f0e8b13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-0.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517751} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ad5b50-59ef-5132-a6fb-4eb47c9fd526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517617} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7da7969-1bfc-5a7a-973b-13d349fbd405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2a3146e0-f24e-5488-9d01-55dfe24a4858" :question/math-academy-id "q-129665" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=2x,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-2z,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=-2y.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1),z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),$ ${f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,-1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1) & =2(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,-1,-1) & =-2(-1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,-1,-1) & =-2(-1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01,y=-1.02$ and $z=-1.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,-1.02,-1.03) & ≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,-1.02+1,-1.03+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨2,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,-0.02,-0.03⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+2(0.01)+2(-0.02)+2(-0.03) \\\\  & ≈-1+0.02-0.04-0.06 \\\\  & ≈-1.08\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "34086e95-3db3-5bf8-9be4-2678ffe5730a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "348d3e63-246e-544f-943d-87eec084c7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2\\pi +4x-\\pi y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517589} {:answer/id #uuid "871ed938-ce33-556f-8ef5-892006482a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈4\\pi -x-2\\pi y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517743} {:answer/id #uuid "89f60499-597c-504c-905d-9aa123018b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈4\\pi -4x-\\pi y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517747} {:answer/id #uuid "8eab8e94-7ae8-562e-b6e4-834aa6cc6887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-\\frac{1}{2}+\\pi -\\pi x+4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517755} {:answer/id #uuid "d43587aa-caee-5e81-afc1-75cb5372bcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517887}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517747} :answer-field/id #uuid "95092209-0ff6-5c1f-932a-fa94ab6826d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf3c18cd-d64f-5eb9-99df-fc0a97727985" :question/math-academy-id "q-65339" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},y-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\, & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=(2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-4, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇\\,f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =-4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi (y-2) \\\\  & =4\\pi -4x-\\pi y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6536adc9-26a3-559e-813d-4e2a00fc2c8f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518229}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7d0aa5-2342-5d6f-a521-a62d6d54725c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x}{108}+\\frac{y}{54}+\\frac{z}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488506} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8e5783-e729-5001-b425-9bd95d7f94c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x}{108}-\\frac{y}{54}-\\frac{z}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488619} {:answer/id #uuid "52aebee7-a1be-5094-b50c-2fecab43978c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x}{108}-\\frac{y}{108}-\\frac{z}{108}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488628} {:answer/id #uuid "8b357635-83ca-53f3-be6f-efc2212bf65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{x}{108}+\\frac{y}{54}+\\frac{z}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488749} {:answer/id #uuid "bac0b568-47cf-5aed-a2d1-d6ae3ec5aafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x}{54}-\\frac{y}{54}-\\frac{z}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488619} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5948ebf-b36f-50ef-996a-235488992a7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d2fd7c0-6fec-5b30-98fe-3b0bf10ba9a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-65338" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,4,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,4,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,4,4)+∇f(2,4,4)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-4,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,4,4)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(2,4,4)=-\\frac{2}{{({2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2})}^{3/2}}=-\\frac{1}{108}, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-\\frac{y}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(2,4,4)=-\\frac{4}{{({2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2})}^{3/2}}=-\\frac{1}{54}, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-\\frac{z}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(2,4,4)=-\\frac{4}{{({2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2})}^{3/2}}=-\\frac{1}{54}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,4,4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,4,4) & =⟨{f}_{x}(2,4,4),{f}_{y}(2,4,4),{f}_{z}(2,4,4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{108},-\\frac{1}{54},-\\frac{1}{54}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,4,4)+∇f(2,4,4)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-4,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+⟨-\\frac{1}{108},-\\frac{1}{54},-\\frac{1}{54}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-4,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{108}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{54}(y-4)-\\frac{1}{54}(z-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x}{108}+\\frac{1}{54}-\\frac{y}{54}+\\frac{4}{54}-\\frac{z}{54}+\\frac{4}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x}{108}-\\frac{y}{54}-\\frac{z}{54}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e047eab3-175f-5735-b074-16a1c776d03c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518256}}] :activity/title "Linearization of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517481}]}
